---
// The home page opens on the commentary (corpus Web Dev Ask "The front page",
// 9 Nov 2026, discharging PFW-035 and PFW-036; the Editor's rulings of 12 Nov
// 2026, "1-3 as recommended"). Top to bottom: the headline and two buttons,
// the published units as cards, the Latest strip, and then the description,
// the Editor's welcome and the About links, unchanged in wording.
//
// NOTHING HERE IS LISTED BY HAND. The volumes, the unit cards, the Watch-the-
// lectures target and the Latest strip are all read from the content
// collections, and `isVisible` gates every one of them, so a draft shows
// nothing and a newly published unit or page appears without an edit here.
//
// The Teaching Aids section that stood here is gone (PFW-035): the aids were
// superseded by the quotation cards on 28 Oct 2026 (Loop Rev 36). The /slides/
// pages themselves stay published and reachable from their commentary pages.
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import {
  SITE,
  commentaryUrl,
  articleUrl,
  lectureUrl,
  lectureThumbUrl,
  normalizeId,
  formatDate,
  isVisible,
} from '../lib/site';

const volumes = (await getCollection('volumes'))
  .filter(isVisible)
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.title.localeCompare(b.data.title));
const allCommentary = (await getCollection('commentary')).filter(isVisible);
const lectures = (await getCollection('lectures')).filter(isVisible);

// Reading order: chapter, then first verse (the unit), then section number.
const byReadingOrder = (
  a: (typeof allCommentary)[number],
  b: (typeof allCommentary)[number]
) =>
  a.data.chapter - b.data.chapter ||
  (a.data.verseStart ?? 0) - (b.data.verseStart ?? 0) ||
  a.data.sectionNumber - b.data.sectionNumber;

// --- The units as cards ----------------------------------------------------
// Each published unit landing of each published volume, in reading order. The
// thumbnail is the unit's Lecture 1 Slide 1 (lectureThumbUrl), shown only
// where the file exists, the same degrade the section pages make: a unit whose
// image has not been copied over yet shows its card without a picture.
const bookDisplayOf = new Map(volumes.map((v) => [v.data.book, v.data.bookDisplay]));
const volumeUnits = volumes.map((v) => ({
  volume: v,
  units: allCommentary
    .filter((e) => e.data.sectionType === 'unit-landing' && e.data.book === v.data.book)
    .sort(byReadingOrder)
    .map((u) => ({
      id: u.id,
      title: u.data.title,
      verses: `${v.data.bookDisplay} ${u.data.passageRef}`,
      thumb: fs.existsSync(path.join(process.cwd(), 'public', 'lectures', `${u.id}.png`))
        ? lectureThumbUrl(u.id)
        : null,
    })),
}));

// --- The two buttons ---------------------------------------------------------
// Read: the first volume's page. Watch: the first unit's Lecture 1, the
// lecture paired to that unit's landing (the Editor's ruling, 12 Nov 2026; the
// site has no lectures index). Either button is omitted rather than pointing
// at a page that is not published.
const firstVolume = volumeUnits[0];
const firstUnit = volumeUnits.flatMap((vu) => vu.units)[0];
const firstLecture =
  firstUnit && lectures.some((l) => normalizeId(l.data.sectionRef.id) === firstUnit.id)
    ? lectureUrl(firstUnit.id)
    : null;

// --- Latest ----------------------------------------------------------------
// The five most recently published commentary pages, newest first, read from
// each page's own `date` (the publication date; the Editor's ruling, 12 Nov
// 2026). Not `revised`, which a conform sweep moves on many older pages at
// once. Pages published the same day fall back to reading order, latest first.
const LATEST_COUNT = 5;
const latest = [...allCommentary]
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf() || byReadingOrder(b, a))
  .slice(0, LATEST_COUNT)
  .map((e) => ({
    id: e.id,
    title: e.data.title,
    unit: `${bookDisplayOf.get(e.data.book) ?? ''} ${e.data.passageRef}`.trim(),
    date: e.data.date,
  }));

// Parent articles only (chapters excluded); gated so the homepage shows an
// Articles entry point only once an article is published, never an empty
// "coming" section.
const articles = (await getCollection('articles'))
  .filter((a) => !a.id.includes('/') && isVisible(a))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());
---
<BaseLayout
  title={SITE.wordmark}
  description="Verse-by-verse commentary on the New Testament at the systematic-theological register, paired with audiovisual lectures and projectable teaching aids. Freely available under CC BY 4.0."
  tier="1"
>
  <div class="reading reading--wide home">
    <header class="home-hero">
      {/* The approved headline is the page's h1. The series name is not set
          again here: the header's subtitle, directly above, already carries
          it, and repeating it pushed the buttons below a phone's fold. */}
      <h1 class="home-hero__headline">
        A verse-by-verse commentary on the New Testament, paired section by
        section with audiovisual lectures.
      </h1>
      {(firstVolume || firstLecture) && (
        <p class="home-hero__actions">
          {firstVolume && (
            <a class="button button--primary" href={commentaryUrl(firstVolume.volume.data.book)}>
              Read the commentary on {firstVolume.volume.data.bookDisplay}
            </a>
          )}
          {firstLecture && (
            <a class="button" href={firstLecture}>Watch the lectures</a>
          )}
        </p>
      )}
    </header>

    {volumeUnits.map((vu) => vu.units.length > 0 && (
      <section class="home-units" aria-labelledby={`units-${vu.volume.data.book}`}>
        <h2 id={`units-${vu.volume.data.book}`}>
          <a href={commentaryUrl(vu.volume.data.book)}>{vu.volume.data.title}</a>
        </h2>
        <ul class="unit-cards">
          {vu.units.map((u) => (
            <li>
              <a class="unit-card" href={commentaryUrl(u.id)}>
                {u.thumb && (
                  <span class="unit-card__frame">
                    <img src={u.thumb} alt="" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy" />
                  </span>
                )}
                <span class="unit-card__verses">{u.verses}</span>
                <span class="unit-card__title">{u.title}</span>
                <span class="unit-card__action">Start reading</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))}

    {latest.length > 0 && (
      <section class="home-latest" aria-labelledby="latest">
        <h2 id="latest">Latest</h2>
        <ol class="latest-list">
          {latest.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a href={commentaryUrl(p.id)}>{p.title}</a>
              <span class="latest-list__meta">
                {p.unit} · <time datetime={p.date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}>{formatDate(p.date)}</time>
              </span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    )}

    <p>
      A verse-by-verse commentary on the New Testament, taken up across the
      range of its writings — each volume carried through to a complete whole.
      The published Commentary is paired, section by section, with audiovisual
      Lectures that teach the same scholarly work — the writing for readers at
      depth, the lectures for teachers and students. Particular questions that
      warrant fuller treatment are developed as scholarly articles, published
      here and submitted for peer review in journals in the field. From the
      same work the series publishes teaching aids — projectable slides, each
      with notes on how to teach from it, for use in church and in class. The
      series is freely available under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
      license.
    </p>
    <p>
      SECNT is also an invitation into a new genre of biblical scholarship. The
      Commentary teaches the text’s own compositional architecture — the
      deliberate shape of the writing — as part of what it sets before the
      reader, not as work kept behind the page. And it is unusual in how it is
      made: every position is adjudicated and locked by a named, accountable
      editor, working in a method explained openly on these pages — the
      human-accountable use of assistance toward which scholarship across many
      fields is already trending. Begin with
      <a href="/architecture-principles/">Architecture Principles</a> and
      <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>, and read not only what the series
      concludes but how it reaches its conclusions.
    </p>

    <blockquote class="editor-welcome">
      <p>But most of all, my prayer is that God will speak to you and give you clarity as you seek to know Him better through our commentaries.</p>
      <footer>— Ronald E. Roades, BA, MA (Wheaton College)<br />Chief Editor, SECNT<br /><a href="mailto:editor@secnt.org">editor@secnt.org</a></footer>
    </blockquote>

    {articles.length > 0 && (
      <Fragment>
        <h2>Articles</h2>
        <ul>
          {articles.map((a) => (
            <li><a href={articleUrl(a.id)}>{a.data.title}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </Fragment>
    )}

    <h2>About the series</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/about/">About SECNT</a></li>
      <li><a href="/architecture-principles/">Architecture Principles</a></li>
      <li><a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a></li>
      <li><a href="/citing/">Citing</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
